  "nl_text": "The database schema named 'schemabench_0073' contains 12 tables. The main purpose of this schema is to manage various aspects of user activities, including authentication, authorization, and session management.\nThe 'activities' table is used to track user activities, including the IP address, user ID, type of activity, occurrence time, and a description. The 'users' table holds information about users, including their user name, email, first name, last name, locale, theme, group ID, verification status, and enabled status. The 'groups' table is used to manage user groups, including their slug, name, description, icon, and creation and update timestamps.\nThe 'password_resets' table is used to manage password resets, including the user ID, hash, completion status, expiration time, completion and update timestamps. The 'verifications' table is used to manage user verifications, including the user ID, hash, completion status, expiration time, completion and update timestamps.\nThe 'persistences' table is used to manage user persistences, including the user ID, token, persistent token, and expiration time. The 'sessions' table is used to manage user sessions, including the session ID, user ID, IP address, user agent, payload, and last activity timestamp.\nThe 'roles' and 'permissions' tables are used to manage user roles and permissions, respectively. The 'role_users' and 'permission_roles' tables are used to manage the relationship between users and roles, and between permissions and roles.\nThe 'throttles' table is used to manage IP-based and request-based throttling, including the type, IP address, request data, and creation and update timestamps.\nAll tables have foreign keys that reference other tables, ensuring referential integrity and maintaining the relationships between tables.",
  "gold_question": "Background / Context\nThe project involves enhancing a user management system named UserFrosting, built on a PHP-based web framework. UserFrosting distinguishes itself by providing a fully-functional user management application that can be easily extended to meet custom requirements using a modular system known as \"Sprinkles.\" This system addresses the modern development needs by offering a comprehensive set of tools for building scalable applications.\n\n Objectives\nThe primary objective of the project is to provide robust user management features, including user authentication, role-based access control (RBAC), and administrative capabilities for managing users. It aims to deliver a maintainable and extendable framework that follows modern development practices.\n\n Stakeholders & Users\n- Administrators: Manage user accounts, permissions, and roles.\n- Developers: Extend and customize the application using Sprinkles.\n- End Users: Register, log in, and interact with the application.\n\n Functional Descriptions\n1. User Authentication: \n   - Users can sign in and maintain persistent sessions with a \"remember me\" feature.\n   \n2. Admin User Management:\n   - Administrators can view, search, sort, create, and edit user accounts, including resetting passwords.\n   - Tools are available to enable or disable users and manage user permissions.\n   \n3. Roles and Permissions:\n   - Utilize powerful RBAC to define access rules and manage permissions through a role management interface.\n   \n4. Extensibility:\n   - Implement custom features using modular Sprinkles without altering the core codebase.\n   - Support for themes, plugins, and more.\n   \n5. User Dashboards:\n   - AdminLTE-themed dashboards for providing users with an interactive interface.\n\n6. Activity Tracking:\n   - Log user activities with details such as IP address and activity type.\n   \n7. Password Management:\n   - Support for password resets and verifications with expiration and completion tracking.\n\n Non-Functional Descriptions\n1. Performance:\n   - Optimization through asset bundling with Node.js and caching for templates and routes.\n   \n2. Documentation:\n   - Comprehensive guides explaining the usage, features, and rationale behind the system components.\n   \n3. Maintainability:\n   - Separation of concerns to minimize code complexity and facilitate future upgrades.\n\n4. Scalability:\n   - Designed to accommodate growth and incorporation of additional user roles and permissions without impacting performance.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_deepseek/0073.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0073\nCREATE TABLE `activities` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `ip_address` varchar(45) NOT NULL,\n  `activity_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `occurrence_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `description` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `activities_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `users` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `first_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `last_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `locale` varchar(10) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `theme` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `group_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `verification_status` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT '0',\n  `enabled_status` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT '1',\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `users_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`group_id`) REFERENCES `groups` (`id`) ON DELETE SET NULL ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `groups` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `slug` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `description` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `icon` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `password_resets` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `hash` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `completed` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT '0',\n  `expiration_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `completed_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `password_resets_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `verifications` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `hash` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `completed` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT '0',\n  `expiration_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `completed_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `verifications_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `persistences` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `token` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `persistent_token` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `expiration_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `persistences_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `sessions` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `session_id` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `ip_address` varchar(45) NOT NULL,\n  `user_agent` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `payload` text NOT NULL,\n  `last_activity` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `sessions_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `roles` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `description` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `permissions` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `description` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `role_users` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `role_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `role_users_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `role_users_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`role_id`) REFERENCES `roles` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `permission_roles` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `permission_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `role_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `permission_roles_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`permission_id`) REFERENCES `permissions` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `permission_roles_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`role_id`) REFERENCES `roles` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `throttles` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `type` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `ip_address` varchar(45) NOT NULL,\n  `request_data` text NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
